A||||5||JohnnyK||clutch engaging at different pedal heights?||||1||
Z||000000||JohnnyK||04-04-2001||03:35 PM||||One stoplight it was nice.. clutch engaged with the pedal almost near the top. Next stop light, .5" from the floor. and then back again. The clutch has 1300 miles on it, and i made sure to sand the shaft down to give it clearance.. what gives?||||reg||1
Z||000001||StuGood||04-04-2001||08:23 PM||||...were you wearing "Pump" sneakers?<P>Think the clutch engaging almost near the top isn't so "nice."  It's supposed to begin engaging fairly close to the floor, ||||reg||12
Z||000002||JohnnyK||04-04-2001||08:34 PM||||Not near the top, I should say in the middle. Fairly high off the floor. 1 inch from the floor is a little underengineering I would say.||||reg||1
Z||000003||MrPBody||04-04-2001||08:35 PM||||My '85 SE 4-cylinder had a similar problem -- the clutch pedal would "pump-up" in traffic, evidently because there was no slack in the linkage between the pedal and the master cylinder.  I bent the pedal return stop back a little so the linkage is no longer tight.  Works fine now.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||sjp777||04-04-2001||09:54 PM||||It's your clutch master cylinder. That is the first sign it is sucking air. It will only get worse until you replace it||||reg||1
Z||000005||qwikgta||04-04-2001||11:04 PM||||My 88 with less than 3000 on the new clutch did the same thing, this lasted for about 3 months until last week when the slave cylinder blew out (strike 1).  Then when I was trying to add fluid to the master cylinder, i broke off the resivor from the piston (strike 2).  Now I have to replace both and im sure this will give me a problem (strike 3).  <P>Rob <P>------------------<BR>qwikgta@yahoo.com<BR>88 T-Top coupe<BR>http://robsfieroproject.homestead.com/index.html||||reg||11
